5
00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:18.219
Depending on your project setup or technical requirements, you may want to take advantage

6
00:00:18.219 --> 00:00:20.260
of sublevels.

7
00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:24.539
Sublevels are simply levels but nested under a persistent level.

8
00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:29.719
Some advantages of using sublevels are asset organization and level segregation,

9
00:00:29.719 --> 00:00:32.759
isolation of level assets based on departments.

10
00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:38.020
Note, if you're using source control, each sublevel can be individually checked out and

11
00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.700
worked on simultaneously.

12
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:44.820
For virtual production-based workflows, you can opt to segregate your content by area

13
00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.700
of expertise or department.

14
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.179
Maintaining organization is crucial for efficiency on any project.

15
00:00:52.179 --> 00:00:57.380
This is especially true for Sequencer workflows, where a well-structured system can prevent

16
00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.740
wasted time and frustration.

17
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.659
Remember to apply organizational principles to your file and folder structure, asset

18
00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:08.780
hierarchy, and overall workflow.

19
00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.719
As previously mentioned, using subscene tracks is a great way to keep assets segregated

20
00:01:13.719 --> 00:01:15.840
and facilitate concurrent work.

21
00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.640
As each subscene track is a separate sequence, this allows multiple users to work on the

22
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.370
main sequence at the same time.

23
00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:27.629
The example on screen has broken the main sequence into separate subscenes based on

24
00:01:27.629 --> 00:01:29.170
studio departments.

25
00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:35.510
Feel free to use the same or different structure based on your project or pipeline needs.

26
00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:40.870
Unreal allows you to create, save, and export layouts, which can then be imported for later

27
00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:41.909
use.

28
00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:46.549
As you become more familiar with Sequencer, you may find that showing or hiding certain

29
